<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Soyo</h1><p class="st-meta">1641 CE – 1885 CE (approx.) · capital Mbanza Soyo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Kongo coastal province at the mouth of the Congo River that broke away to become an independent Catholic principality and defeated Portugal in 1670.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soyo">Soyo</a> was a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kingdom of Kongo</a>, ruled by a count (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a> Soyo) from Mbanza Soyo, close to the river mouth. Its control of the port of Mpinda and direct trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> made it rich, and in 1641 Count Daniel da Silva asserted independence from the Kongo king.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soyo">Soyo's</a> counts, later styled princes, ruled as Catholic monarchs, hosting Italian Capuchin missionaries. Trade in slaves, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English merchants funded a strong army. In 1670 Soyo defeated a Portuguese invasion at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kitombo">Battle of Kitombo</a>, and it later intervened in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a> civil wars.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soyo">Soyo</a> remained autonomous through the 18th and 19th centuries, though its power waned with the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. It was incorporated into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-angola">Portuguese Angola</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-conference">Berlin Conference</a> of 1884–85. The town of Soyo is now an Angolan oil centre.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1641 CE – 1885 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.36%;width:4.86%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1641 CE – 1885 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mbanza Soyo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Daniel da Silva</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kikongo</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Angola</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Soyo, County of Soyo</dd></dl></aside></div>
